<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · island</p><h1 class="enc-title">Hispaniola</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Ayiti, Quisqueya</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Hispaniola is the second-largest island of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caribbean">Caribbean</a>, in the Greater Antilles, shared today by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haiti">Haiti</a> in the west and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dominican-republic">Dominican Republic</a> in the east. Called Ayiti by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taino">Taíno</a>, it was reached by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christopher-columbus">Columbus</a> in 1492 and became the first base of Spanish colonisation in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/americas">Americas</a>, with its capital at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/santo-domingo">Santo Domingo</a>. Its western third became the French colony of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-domingue">Saint-Domingue</a> in 1697.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hispaniola.jpg" alt="Hispaniola" width="320" height="218" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Earth Science and Remote Sensing Unit, Lyndon B. Johnson Space Center · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:ISS027-E-17333_-_View_of_Dominican_Republic.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Hispaniola lies in the Greater Antilles between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuba">Cuba</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/puerto-rico">Puerto Rico</a>, about 80 kilometres east of Cuba across the Windward Passage and 130 kilometres west of Puerto Rico across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mona-passage">Mona Passage</a>. It covers 76,192 square kilometres, of which 27,750 belong to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haiti">Haiti</a> and the rest to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dominican-republic">Dominican Republic</a>. The northeast trade winds drop their rain on the northern slopes and leave a dry southern coast. The Hispaniolan hutia and the Hispaniolan solenodon are its two endemic land mammals.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>People of the Archaic Age reached the island from Central or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-america">South America</a> about 6,000 years ago. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taino">Taíno</a>, descended from Arawakan speakers who migrated into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caribbean">Caribbean</a> in the 1st millennium BCE, were the dominant group at European contact. Each society was a small kingdom under a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cacique">cacique</a>, and in 1492 five kingdoms ruled the island. Early chroniclers recorded its native names as Bohío, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haiti">Haiti</a> and Quisqueya, though doubts have been raised about Quisqueya.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christopher-columbus">Christopher Columbus</a> landed in December 1492 and named it Española, or little <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>. His flagship Santa Maria ran aground on 24 December, and he left 21 men in the fort of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/la-navidad">La Navidad</a>. Hispaniola became the site of the first European fort, the first settlement, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/la-isabela">La Isabela</a>, and the first permanent settlement, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/santo-domingo">Santo Domingo</a>, founded in 1498. Gold mining with forced Indigenous labour began early. In 1606 Philip III ordered the island's inhabitants to move close to Santo Domingo to fight piracy, but French, English and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> pirates then set up bases on the north and west coasts.</p><p>The French occupied the west and built a plantation colony on the enslavement of Africans that became the most prosperous of the French empire in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/americas">Americas</a>. A slave revolt broke out in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-domingue">Saint-Domingue</a> in 1791, and by the Treaty of Basel in 1795 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> ceded the eastern two thirds to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haitian-revolution">Haitian Revolution</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jean-jacques-dessalines">Jean-Jacques Dessalines</a> proclaimed the Empire of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haiti">Haiti</a>, and in 1809 Spain regained the east. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> occupied Haiti from 1915 to 1934 and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dominican-republic">Dominican Republic</a> from 1916 to 1924.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The island is divided between the Spanish-speaking Dominican Republic in the east, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/santo-domingo">Santo Domingo</a> as its capital, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haiti">Haiti</a> in the west, where <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haitian-creole">Haitian Creole</a> is spoken by nearly the whole population. The name Hispaniola became widely used after 1918, when American leaders recommended it to the National Geographic Society.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Hispaniola” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The western part of <b>Hispaniola</b>, Saint-Domingue, passed formally to France in 1697 and became the most profitable sugar colony in the Caribbean.</blockquote><a href="/empire/french-empire">French Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Haiti, the Taíno name for <b>Hispaniola</b>, was reclaimed in 1804 by Jean-Jacques Dessalines for the state the enslaved people of French Saint-Domingue had won.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-haiti">Republic of Haiti · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>In February 1822 he occupied Santo Domingo, which had just declared independence from Spain, bringing the whole of <b>Hispaniola</b> under the republic and abolishing slavery there.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-haiti-1806-1849">Republic of Haiti (1806-1849) · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Spain held all of <b>Hispaniola</b> from the 1490s, but as its interest shifted to the mainland the western part was neglected.</blockquote><a href="/empire/saint-domingue">Saint-Domingue · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>After Christopher Columbus’s expedition founded a Spanish colony on <b>Hispaniola</b> in 1492 and the Taíno population was decimated, colonists began importing enslaved West Africans around 1512; some were priests of African traditional religions, others had already absorbed Catholic influences or were Muslim.</blockquote><a href="/religion/haitian-vodou">Haitian Vodou · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>The people now called the Taíno lived across most of the West Indies when Europeans arrived, from the Lucayan Archipelago through Cuba, Jamaica, <b>Hispaniola</b> and Puerto Rico to Guadeloupe.</blockquote><a href="/culture/taino">Taíno · Origins</a></li></ul></section>
